## 🪪 Part A — The Model Card Template

*One card per model or AI system. Lives with the system and in the governance repo. Versioned.*

### 1. Identity
- **Model / system name & version**
- **Owner** (accountable person) and **team**
- **Date created / last updated**
- **Linked AIA ID** (Day 35) and **Risk Register IDs** (Day 34)
- **Lifecycle status:** Development / Staging / Production / Deprecated

### 2. Intended Use
- **Primary intended use** and **intended users**
- **Out-of-scope / prohibited uses** (be explicit — this bounds liability)
- **Decisions the system makes or influences**

### 3. Model Details
- **Type / architecture** (e.g., fine-tuned LLM, gradient-boosted classifier, RAG pipeline)
- **Base/foundation model & vendor** (if applicable) + version pinning
- **Inputs and outputs** (formats, ranges)
- **Key hyperparameters / configuration** relevant to behavior

### 4. Data
- **Training / fine-tuning data:** sources, size, time range, provenance, licensing
- **Retrieval/knowledge sources** (for RAG)
- **Personal / special-category data** used and legal basis
- **Known data limitations & representativeness gaps**

### 5. Performance
- **Evaluation metrics** and results on held-out / real-world data
- **Evaluation dataset** description (and how it differs from production)
- **Performance across subgroups** (not just aggregate — this is where fairness hides)
- **Confidence / uncertainty behavior**

### 6. Fairness & Bias
- **Protected groups considered**
- **Fairness metric(s) used** and results (e.g., disparate impact ratio)
- **Known biases and mitigations applied**

### 7. Limitations & Failure Modes
- **Where the model is known to fail** (edge cases, distribution shift, hallucination patterns)
- **Adversarial robustness** notes
- **Drift/decay expectations** and monitoring plan

### 8. Human Oversight & Controls
- **Human-in/on-the-loop** design
- **Override / escalation / kill-switch** mechanics
- **Guardrails** (input/output filtering, rate limits, scoping)

### 9. Ethical & Regulatory
- **EU AI Act risk classification**
- **Explainability approach** (how a decision is explained to an affected person)
- **Environmental / compute cost** (optional but increasingly expected)

